GBA Rush PSP 1.4.0 design
=========================

Status
------

Approved for specification on 22 August 2026. This file is a temporary
development record. It is removed from the release branch before 1.4.0 is
tagged; the committed revision remains available in Git history.

Objective
---------

Version 1.4.0 is a quality release. It improves fast-forward behavior, ports
individually justified gpSP corrections, raises audio quality where PSP
performance allows it, strengthens regression coverage, and reduces public
documentation to four Markdown files.

New graphics features, overlays, advanced savestate management, a core rebase,
new cheat formats, and wider link-cable modes are outside this release.

Fast-forward policy
-------------------

A portable fast_forward_policy module owns the complete fast-forward state.
Input, audio, video, menus, power handling, and link handling consume its
decisions instead of changing synchronize_flag independently.

The policy receives:

- the selected global profile and optional per-game override;
- button press, hold, and release events;
- emulated-frame count and a monotonic clock;
- menu, ROM, reset, power, exit, and link-session transitions;
- measured presentation and emulation headroom.

It returns:

- active state and requested speed;
- whether the current frame should be presented;
- the remaining delay before the next emulation deadline for limited speeds;
- the selected audio policy;
- the short status text and diagnostic counters.

Profiles
--------

Modern is the default and preserves the 1.3.0 interaction: hold the assigned
button for the maximum available speed. Audio is silent. Presentation is
time-based at 30 updates per second so rendering cannot grow with emulation
speed.

Classic is a toggle cycle: Normal, 2x, 3x, Normal. The 2x and 3x values are
real timing targets rather than labels derived only from frameskip. Audio is
silent. Presentation targets 60 updates per second by showing one of every two
or three emulated frames.

Custom exposes:

- activation: Hold or Toggle;
- speed: 2x, 3x, or Maximum;
- presentation: Smooth, Balanced, or Maximum performance;
- audio: Silent or Accelerated.

Smooth, Balanced, and Maximum performance target 60, 30, and 15 presented
frames per second respectively. Limited 2x and 3x modes use deadline pacing
without waiting for a display VBlank on every emulated frame. Maximum remains
unthrottled.

Accelerated audio is never used by Modern or Classic. If it cannot keep pace,
Custom falls back to silence for that fast-forward session and records the
reason in diagnostics. Accelerated audio is available at 2x and 3x, where
each fixed group of two or three stereo frames is averaged into one real-time
output frame with integer arithmetic. Maximum forces silence because its speed
is neither fixed nor stable enough for a bounded resampler.

Configuration and interface
---------------------------

The global profile is stored in the versioned global configuration. Per-game
configuration stores either Use global or a complete override. Loading a
1.3.x configuration selects Modern without changing any existing numeric
setting or button mapping.

Invalid new fields fall back independently. An invalid per-game override
becomes Use global and does not invalidate unrelated game settings.

The Performance menu shows the profile, per-game override, and custom fields.
Custom fields remain visible but unavailable when another profile is active.
Contextual help states the effective activation, speed, presentation, and
audio behavior.

The normal overlay uses one compact value: FF 2.00x, FF 3.00x, or FF MAX.
Diagnostics also show requested and measured speed, emulated FPS, presented
FPS, transition count, and audio fallback count.

Lifecycle rules
---------------

Entering a menu, changing or resetting a ROM, suspending, exiting, and opening
a link session disable fast-forward and clear every toggle latch. Resume never
restores a pre-suspend fast-forward state.

Leaving fast-forward discards queued turbo audio, resets pacing history, and
forces the next frame to be presented. A failed audio recovery leaves gameplay
running silently and permits a later normal recovery attempt.

Limited modes sleep only when emulation is ahead of their deadline. If a game
cannot reach 2x or 3x, the scheduler never adds delay and diagnostics report the
measured speed instead of displaying a false multiplier.

Fast-forward performs no allocation, formatting, file access, or report write
in an emulation, rendering, or audio hot path. Clock arithmetic is wrap-safe
and all rate decisions can run against a deterministic test clock.

Core and audio backports
------------------------

The current libretro gpSP tree is a reference, not a merge source. Each
candidate change is classified as already equivalent, locally applicable, not
relevant to the PSP fork, or deferred. A locally applicable change requires a
regression test before the production edit.

The first review covers:

- recursive open-bus reads while executing from unmapped memory;
- dynarec external-branch resolution and host-stack growth;
- ARM BX PC behavior and I/O write mirroring;
- failure to allocate a usable gamepak buffer;
- rotated-bitmap mosaic correctness and avoidable modulo work;
- integer-only fixed-point audio seeds and correct interpolation rounding;
- a higher-precision audio mix bus.

The higher-precision mix bus is accepted only if its memory use does not reduce
the usable ROM buffer and its added arithmetic does not regress PSP-2000
throughput or introduce underruns. Correct changes that cannot meet the PSP
resource gate are documented in the 1.4.0 release notes and deferred.

Performance gates
-----------------

The 1.3.0 release build is the baseline. Candidate and baseline use the same
PSP-2000, Memory Stick, ROM, savestate, clock, renderer, and configuration.
Each measurement uses one warm-up followed by three recorded runs; medians are
compared.

- 2x and 3x measured emulation speed stay within three percent of target.
- Maximum is not slower than 1.3.0.
- Normal-speed throughput does not regress by more than two percent.
- Normal-speed 95th-percentile frame duration does not regress by more than
  five percent.
- Releasing fast-forward creates no audio backlog or repeated skipped-frame
  burst.
- Cheats, saves, suspend, and link-disabled play retain their current behavior.

Metrics include emulated and presented FPS, frame-time percentiles, GE wait,
audio underruns and recoveries, dynarec compilations and flushes, and cache
high-water marks.

Verification
------------

Portable tests cover every state transition for Hold and Toggle, every profile,
speed limiting, clock wrap, forced shutdown events, audio fallback, and return
to normal pacing. Configuration tests cover 1.3.x migration, global and
per-game round trips, malformed fields, interrupted writes, and recovery.

Every core backport has a focused regression case. The full host suite runs
with warnings as errors and again under AddressSanitizer and
UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer. Source checks protect lifecycle ownership, hot-path
constraints, translation ordering, the release manifest, and documentation
limits. Normal and diagnostic PSP builds use PSPDEV v20260801.

Hardware acceptance on PSP-2000 with Pokemon FireRed in Italian covers cold
start, load, save, cheats, all fast-forward profiles, menu transitions, clean
fast-forward exit, ten suspend cycles, and normal XMB exit. PSP-1000, PSP-3000,
PSP Go, PSP Street, a second PSP, and the wider game corpus are optional for
the release and are reported precisely when available.

Repository structure
--------------------

The 1.4.0 release branch contains exactly four tracked Markdown files:

- README.md: install, upgrade, operation, cheats, link, diagnostics, and
  concise lineage;
- CHANGELOG.md: version history;
- CONTRIBUTING.md: native and Docker builds, tests, style, backports, and
  release procedure;
- source/single-game_launcher/tool/README.md: the independent launcher tool.

The docs directory, BUILD-DOCKER.md, dated design and review material, and
per-release Markdown files are removed after their unique current information
is consolidated. Legal notices, third-party terms, and source offers remain in
plain-text license or notice files. Git history and GitHub releases retain the
historical record.

Automated checks require exactly four Markdown files, reject broken internal
links and duplicate release documentation, and scan tracked content and commit
metadata for unwanted generator attribution. This DESIGN-1.4.0 file is removed
before the release commit.

Release conditions
------------------

Version metadata remains 1.4.0-dev until all automated gates, the PSP-2000
acceptance run, archive inspection, checksum verification, and public-content
checks pass. The release package contains only EBOOT.PBP, exception.prx,
installation text, changelog, and license. It contains no ROM, BIOS, save,
savestate, cheat list, box art, benchmark report, input trace, or user
configuration.

